  "query_preview": "..\n\nAdditional Snowfall through Monday morning...\n\nSnow will continue across much of Southwest through North Central \nMontana as moisture from the remnants of Rachel streams north and \neast from the Desert Southwest/Great Basin and over the Northern \nHigh Plains through Monday morning. Latest Hi-Res Ensemble Forecast \n(HREF) guidance suggests that snowfall rates of between 0.25-0.5\"\nper hour will be common from the afternoon hours today through \nearly morning hours on Monday across Southwest and portions of \nCentral Montana as strong ascent associated with a shortwave \nembedded within the southwest flow aloft lifts north and over the \nNorthern Rockies. There are even  that northerly upslope areas \ncould see rates approach 1\" per hour, mainly between 6PM and \nmidnight tonight. Most areas along and north of I-90 and south of \nthe Montana Hwy 200 corridor can expect to see 4\" to 8\" of \nadditional snowfall through Monday morning, with amounts near 12\" \nin the northern slopes/foothills of the Rocky Mountain Front, \nIsland Ranges of Central Montana, and Elkhorn and Boulder \nMountains between 3500- 6500ft. \n\nGiven the additional heavy snow across these areas the Winter \nWeather Advisory for Fergus County below 4500ft, Helena Valley, \nMissouri Headwaters, and Madison River Valley was upgraded to a \nWinter Storm Warning. Additional tree damage and isolated power \noutages can be expected in areas covered by a Winter Storm Warning \ngiven the heavy, wet nature of the snow.\n\nCold Temperatures Monday night/Tuesday morning...\n\nClearing skies, light winds, and a relatively fresh snowpack will \nresult in the coldest temperatures of the upcoming week, with the \nlow end of guidance suggesting that lows could fall into the upper \nsingle digits above zero. - Moldan",
